      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Japanese College of Cardiology. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The treatment strategy for in-stent restenosis (ISR) with bifurcation lesions has not been well explored. We examined the clinical outcomes between final kissing balloon technique (FKBT) after stent implantation and single-stent implantation without FKBT for bifurcation ISR lesions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We identified 115 consecutive ISR with bifurcation lesions among 108 patients who underwent drug-eluting stent implantation. The patients were divided into the FKBT group (34 patients, 35 lesions) and the non-FKBT group (74 patients, 80 lesions)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Thrombolysis in myocardial infarction flow grade of side branch was significantly greater in the patients with FKBT than those without FKBT after coronary intervention (2.80±0.46 vs. 2.65±0.68, p=0.04), but this difference was attenuated and was no longer statistically significant at the time of follow-up (2.80±0.48 vs. 2.80±0.60, p=0.97). During a mean follow-up of 47.8±23.6 months, there were no significant differences in the incidence of major adverse cardiac events (MACE). In multivariate analysis, estimated glomerular filtration rate (hazard ratio: 0.96, 95% confidence interval: 0.92-0.99, p=0.02) was an independent predictor of MACE. Contrast volume (170.71±47.17ml vs. 136.46±55.56ml, p=0.002) and radiation dose (1.44±1.65Gy vs. 0.96±0.46Gy, p=0.02) were significantly higher in the FKBT group than in the non-FKBT group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Single-stent implantation without FKBT may be a sufficient treatment strategy for bifurcation ISR lesions."
          }
        ]
      },
